WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.476
JEFF DAVIS: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Platform Education

00:00:02.476 --> 00:00:03.360
Series.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.710
My name is Jeff Davis.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.140
And in this session we'll
discuss building applications

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.370
that scale automatically
with Google App Engine.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.440
We'll be running a moderated
Q&amp;A during the session,

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.590
so feel free to ask
questions using the Ask

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:20.220
Your Question Here field on
the screen in front of you.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.630
Let's get started.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:24.570
So the format of this
session will be-- we'll

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.740
start with a demo.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.680
And this is an in-depth demo
of a relatively sophisticated

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.119
application that will show
the magic of App Engine

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.400
in automatically
scaling to meet demand,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.980
as well as how easy it is
to manage your application

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.720
once it's in production.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.090
Coming out of the
demonstration, we

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.830
will have a
high-level discussion

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.230
about the range of Google
App Engine features

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.177
and how best to leverage them.

00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:50.010
We'll take a few minutes
to talk about where

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.150
you can get more information
online after this session.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.640
We'll talk about best
practices for evaluating

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.520
Google App Engine for specific
use cases in your organization.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.940
And again, we'll be doing
a running Q&amp;A. Feel free

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.110
to ask questions as we go.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.630
So let's cut over to a demo.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.860
This is a customer success
application running

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.730
on top of Google App Engine.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:17.030
We work for a company
called betterdev.io.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.730
And betterdev makes--
among other things--

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.770
a videoconferencing application.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.300
So here we have a mock-up of a
video conferencing application.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.790
And users can perform calls.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.160
So this is a one-way call.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.340
You can actually see me
in the studio right now.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.110
When they hang up,
it's going to record

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.170
information about this call
into a backend database.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.100
And what we're really
interested in as a vendor is we

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.030
want to make sure that we know
all the ways that our users

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.920
interact with our product.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.660
And then for each
customer, we want

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.150
to know where there
are opportunities

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.870
to improve their outcomes
and optimize our chances

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:59.960
at renewals and upsells.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.170
So here we have a customer
success application.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.227
This is being used by betterdev.

00:02:06.227 --> 00:02:08.060
They've actually bought
it as a SaaS product

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.000
from another company
called Success-hq.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.640
But you can see this dashboard
gives the customer success

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.890
managers at betterdev insight
into their various customers.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.690
They have a list
of projects here

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.060
that are underway
that they maybe

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.450
should be paying attention to,
they have upcoming renewals,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.900
and they have trending changes
in usage and engagement

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:31.650
with the products.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.858
So you can see, for instance,
that the calls per week

00:02:33.858 --> 00:02:38.430
for Skills Feed-- one of our
customers-- has gone up by 30%.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.340
If you click on
any of these, you

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.240
can actually get
a dashboard that

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.580
gives you a whole
bunch of detail

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.430
about how users
within this customer

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.750
are actually
leveraging the product.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.740
So for instance, the
Team Davis account,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.710
they've purchased 10
video conferencing devices

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.000
to go with the software.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.870
They have an annual contract
value of roughly $25,000

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:04.710
a year.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.920
And we can see the trends
in their engagement

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.400
with the product.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.930
So when I refresh the page,
this is actually loaded live

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:17.040
from the backend data that's
been recorded as users interact

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.130
with the product.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:21.390
So we have purchased
devices, registered devices,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:25.140
registered users, percentage
of devices provisioned,

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.350
calls per week, stuff like that.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:31.030
Now what a customer
success manager

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.760
would do with this is they
would look for opportunities

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.250
to improve the customer's
engagement with the products.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:40.090
So for instance, the first
thing that we notice is only 60%

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.910
of the purchased devices have
actually been registered.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.670
We should get those other
ones plugged in and going.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.980
We also notice that
the calls per week

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.110
is trending down over time.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.020
So this shows the last 30 days.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.440
And we actually want to boost
this up, not have it go down.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.010
On the other hand, we can see
that support ticket volume has

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:04.060
gone down and end of call
ratings are slightly going up.

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.010
So those are positive
things that we

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:07.840
could bring up in a
quarterly business

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.820
review with the customer.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:13.860
Now, this is super helpful
when running a customer success

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.339
organization, and we
want this application

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:18.930
to serve our needs as we grow.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.209
As our customer
success organization

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:24.120
gets bigger and
bigger, we want to be

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.740
able to serve more
and more users.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.300
And as we have more and more end
users of our videoconferencing

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.610
product, we want
all of that data

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.520
to be able to be
ingested into the backend

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.280
and thus rendered in
widgets on these dashboard

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.040
without any operations
overhead on our part.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.250
So let's take a look.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.310
All of these charts
are being pulled out

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.110
of a backend data repository.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.270
We're using Google
BigQuery, which

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.290
is Google's petabyte-scale,
low-cost data warehousing

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.280
solution.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.230
You can see that
we actually have

00:04:58.230 --> 00:04:59.790
a table of all of the events.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.870
So we have the date,
the type of event--

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.010
whether it's a call or a page
load or a comment or a dial-in

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:05.940
session--

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.520
who did it, what company
they work for, and then

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.820
some other information.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:11.320
If you look down
here at the bottom,

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:12.403
you can see that we have--

00:05:12.403 --> 00:05:17.340
525,000 events
have been recorded.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.540
Now, I was just doing
one call at a time,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.570
and so that's writing
one event at a time

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.390
into this backend
data warehouse.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.550
But what happens if there
are hundreds or thousands

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.320
or tens of thousands of users?

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.180
We want to make sure that our
application doesn't fall over.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:34.860
But I'm not a real expert when
it comes to doing operations,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.650
so I would like Google to take
care of all of that for me.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:41.420
And just to show that this is
actually all on the up and up,

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.170
let me go back-- and
I'm going to bring up

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.990
the betterdev dashboard.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:51.090
And you can see that this is
showing that we've done 2,600

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.790
calls roughly in the last week.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:06:00.331
If I go back to this UI
and I do a new call--

00:06:00.331 --> 00:06:06.580
hi-- and then I hang up,
if I refresh this view,

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:08.770
it should actually show 2,633.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:11.080
So you can see that it updated.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:15.820
My use of the product flowed
event data into the backend,

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.550
and then that backend
is being rendered

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.290
in a nice visualization here.

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:24.310
So let's take a look
at the architecture.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.140
What we have here is customers.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:33.130
These are users of the
videoconferencing software.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:37.030
And every time that
they use the product,

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.920
it's going to write an event
to an event API service.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.680
So this is an API,
and it runs separately

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.680
from the frontend of the
customer success software that

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.150
shows the visualizations.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:54.040
And all it does is record
data into BigQuery.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.630
Now, again, if we have
lots and lots of users,

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.210
we may need lots
and lots of servers

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:00.790
to catch all the
incoming events and write

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.080
those events into BigQuery.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.310
Then the folks who are using
the Success-hq product--

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:08.410
these are the CSMs--

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:12.460
they actually hit
a web application

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:15.060
which renders the frontend
as well as all the data

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.260
visualizations.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:20.250
And this frontend can read data
from a memcache service, which

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.750
is a stored in-memory data
store that is shared across all

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.310
of the frontend servers.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.550
It reads and writes information
about users and companies

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.820
into Cloud Datastore,
which is a NoSQL database.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.070
And it reads all of the
necessary information

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:39.330
to render the dashboard
widgets from BigQuery.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:42.180
This architecture shows
you one thing right away,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.600
which is that App Engine is a
great platform for stitching

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.510
together multiple backend
services from within Google.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:52.260
So you want to use git datastore
and you want to use BigQuery

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.240
and you want to use memcache?

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:55.140
No problem.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.510
App Engine is a great place
to stitch those together.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:01.380
Another thing that we'll see is
I've actually broken my product

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.450
into two different services.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.180
These aren't quite
micro services,

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.730
they're sort of macro services,
but this gives you the idea

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.650
that you can actually
split your application

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.510
into discrete
components and then we

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.890
can manage those
components separately.

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:19.050
Let's take a look at what's
required to actually put this

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.600
all together in production.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.320
So behind the scenes,
here we have App Engine.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:29.560
And you can see that in
the App Engine console,

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.190
I've got my two different
services running.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:35.890
So I've got the API events
service and the default

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:38.890
service, which is
my web frontend.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:41.659
And you can see that
I've got instances

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:42.950
that are automatically running.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.550
So for the frontend,
App Engine is currently

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.260
running two services.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.960
And if you look
at the API events,

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.960
you can see that it's
currently running one service.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.450
What would be great
is if when nobody's

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.460
using either these
of services, it

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.200
would spin down
to zero instances,

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.330
and therefore it doesn't
charge me anything at all.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.390
But then I want it
to scale up to dozens

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:09.310
of servers or instances
if the load requires that.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.630
And in fact, let's try that out.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.800
Let's just see how this
autoscaling stuff works.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.951
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to use my SaaS growth

00:09:18.951 --> 00:09:19.450
generator.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:23.410
If only it was actually just
this simple to generate growth

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:24.700
in a SaaS business.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.940
So I'm going to start with
generating a low background

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:29.420
load of new events.

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.510
So this is emulating
a bunch of people

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:33.280
using the video
conferencing product,

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.700
and it's writing events
into the backend.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:38.590
What I should see here--

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:40.660
if I look at the
events service--

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.570
is I'm going to stay--

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.642
you'll notice I've
got 27 requests.

00:09:44.642 --> 00:09:46.600
There's a little bit of
delay in updating this,

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.790
but you're going to start seeing
that those request numbers are

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:49.290
going up.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:54.430
So it's 40 requests, 45
requests, 50 requests.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:58.190
And we're averaging fractions
of a query per second.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:04.270
This is easily handleable
within the single instance

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.380
that we have running.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:08.990
So we can look at latency,
we can look at errors,

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.560
we can look at traffic
or utilization,

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:12.910
that sort of thing.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:15.250
So not a ton of load.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:17.290
Let's go back to our
magic load generator

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.230
and say, hey, now we've
got product market fit,

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:23.614
we've got 10,000 simultaneous
users at any given time.

00:10:23.614 --> 00:10:25.030
I'm going to click
on this button.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:27.430
This is going to emulate
lots more users using

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:30.470
the product, thus lots more
events being written in.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:33.430
Now, I haven't had
to do anything,

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:36.490
but what we should
see fairly shortly

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.730
here is additional
instances getting

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.990
spun up because the
queries per second goes up.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.030
So we can see it
continues to go up.

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:51.654
It hasn't spun up
another instance yet.

00:10:51.654 --> 00:10:53.330
So, so far, so good.

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:55.340
Well, let's just
drop the hammer.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:58.580
Let's go straight to a
million simultaneous users.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:01.400
This is a lot of
calls on my API--

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.610
clearly more than one
instance can handle.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.590
Now you'll notice I've
got two instances.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.340
And there we go.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.030
So now you see we've
got five instances.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:19.970
And this will keep growing
out as is required.

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:25.220
So App Engine is using an
algorithm of CP utilization

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:30.170
memory utilization, and requests
per second to identify how many

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:32.060
frontend instances--
or in this case,

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:35.060
how many backend API
service instances--

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:40.220
I need in order to service
all the incoming requests.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:43.880
So we promised you apps
that scale automatically.

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:46.970
Here you go--
automatic app scaling.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:49.280
In addition to
making it really easy

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.490
to scale up or down
depending on load,

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:54.230
App Engine also
makes it really easy

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.670
to manage services
that are in production.

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.940
So let's say I want
to actually release

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:02.030
a new version of the
frontend One of the things

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.480
that I have found is some
people aren't entirely sure what

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.050
to do with this dashboard view.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:10.430
And I'd like to give
them the opportunity

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:15.170
to ask questions of the support
team while they're using it,

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.760
so I need to make
a modification.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:21.550
What I'm going to do is
switch to my code editor.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:24.710
And I'm going to integrate
a third-party product.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.550
So this is a product
that actually

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:30.190
puts a little messenger
box onto a web page

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:32.780
and allows the end user
to talk to somebody

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:34.310
within our organization.

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:36.370
So I'm going to
copy this and I'm

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:42.906
going to paste it into
the customer view.

00:12:42.906 --> 00:12:45.405
What we're going to do is we're
going to put that down here.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.520
Now, this is a new version
of the application,

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:53.290
so I want to update the
versioning information.

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:56.390
I'm going to say
this is version 1-2.

00:12:56.390 --> 00:13:01.390
Now, this is version 1-2 of
the frontend web app service.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.860
Notice there is an API
event service as well.

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:07.190
And I'm going to leave
it configured as 1-1.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.920
So because I have my app
broken into two services,

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.830
I can upgrade one
component without touching

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:15.320
the other components.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:18.440
So now that I've got
that, what I'm going to do

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:21.440
is actually deploy the service.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.390
So here's my command line.

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:30.890
And I'm going to switch into--

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:39.710
let's see-- Success-hq
and I'm going to deploy.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.280
Now, this is all I
have to do to deploy.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:43.990
So it's one simple command line.

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:46.480
It's taking all of
my business logic

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.730
and all of my
application code and it's

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:52.870
loading it up on top of the
App Engine infrastructure.

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:56.290
It's automatically
creating a new version

00:13:56.290 --> 00:14:01.300
of the application frontend
keeping the old version.

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:03.060
So I have a 1.1
version and you'll

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:05.670
see that I have a 1.2
version running now.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:10.330
So what's interesting here is
I go back and I go to versions.

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:12.420
And you'll notice that
for my API event service,

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.640
I only have the one version
because I haven't updated it.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.181
But if I go to
default, you'll notice

00:14:16.181 --> 00:14:17.400
that there are two versions.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.577
You've got the big
blue line down here

00:14:19.577 --> 00:14:20.910
and the big gray line down here.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:24.270
What this means is that
all of the traffic going

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:25.950
to my application
is being served

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.460
from the initial version.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.110
Currently, none of
the traffic is being

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:32.060
served by the new version.

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:35.070
So we've actually
staged this version.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:38.480
People can only get to it if
they use a special URL which

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.490
prepends the version number.

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:43.430
This allows me to test it
in a production environment

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:47.150
without actually having it
be live to my end users.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:49.280
I can also choose
to split traffic.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.620
So, for instance,
if I click on this

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:53.180
and I choose Migrate
Traffic, this

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:55.970
will actually move all of the
traffic from the old version

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.140
to the new version.

00:14:57.140 --> 00:14:58.910
Or I can choose
to split traffic,

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.380
and this would
allow me to say, you

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:03.830
know what, I want
20% of my end users

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:05.840
to be directed to
the new version

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:08.630
while 80% continue to
go to the old version.

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:10.580
This would be a
great way to test

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:14.130
the efficacy of that new
widget that we put in, which

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:15.770
I'll show you in just a sec.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:18.830
All right, so let's
try the new version.

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:20.150
I'm going to click here.

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.400
It's going to open
a special URL.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:26.214
And you'll notice I have
a new capability built in.

00:15:26.214 --> 00:15:27.630
This is asking me
for a free trial

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:29.550
because I haven't paid for this.

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:31.800
But you'll notice in the
bottom right-hand corner now,

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.720
I've got this
messaging window which

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:37.470
allows the end user
ostensibly to talk

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:39.029
to somebody on the backend.

00:15:39.029 --> 00:15:41.070
If I click through, you'll
see that this actually

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:42.470
loads on every single page.

00:15:46.494 --> 00:15:48.940
I'll dismiss the
free trial thing.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:53.410
And it's having a little
bit of difficulty pulling up

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:57.100
the messaging, but you can see
that the widget actually works.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:01.210
If I go back and I try
loading the first version,

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:03.460
you'll notice that I don't
get the little plug-in.

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:05.020
So I've got two
different versions

00:16:05.020 --> 00:16:07.240
running at the same time.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:10.150
And right now everybody
will be directed

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.910
to the default version.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:17.260
If I want some people to try
the newly installed feature,

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.900
I can send them a
special URL, or, again,

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:23.500
what I can do is I can
do traffic splitting.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:27.132
I've been showing you this
running in a canned version

00:16:27.132 --> 00:16:28.840
that I had already
gotten up and running.

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:30.820
What if I wanted to
deploy this from scratch?

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:32.620
You're like, Jeff,
OK, it's really easy

00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:34.529
once it's up and
running, but maybe it

00:16:34.529 --> 00:16:36.820
was really hard to get up
and going in the first place.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:38.770
And again, not so.

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:40.960
Let's take you
through the process

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:44.450
of deploying this from scratch.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:47.090
So I'm going to
create a new project,

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:52.740
and we're going to
call this success-hq-3.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:56.182
And we're going to
bill it to my account.

00:16:56.182 --> 00:16:57.765
We're going to create
the new project.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.380
Remember, a project in
Google Cloud Platform

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:07.260
is a collection of
applications and resources

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.770
that have a particular
billing account

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:11.890
and a particular
security boundary.

00:17:11.890 --> 00:17:15.119
So I'm going to create a
separate group of resources

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:16.530
for this part of the demo.

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:18.030
And then what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:20.010
going to make a new
App Engine application.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:28.140
So I'm opening up the
Google Cloud Shell,

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:31.350
which is a web UI that has
all the command line tools

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:35.910
that I need to manage
Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:40.780
So I'm going to create a new
application for this project.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:43.670
And it asks me where I want
to locate the application,

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:47.940
and I'm going to put this
in the US central region.

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:56.520
So it takes just a moment to
fire up the new application,

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.110
but it's an empty application.

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.620
There's no code there.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:02.330
So now I need to deploy my code.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.040
So while that's going
in the background,

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:08.540
what I'm going to do is I'm
going to go into my code

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:14.660
and I'm going to change
this to be success-hq-3

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:18.540
and I'm going to change
this to be success-hq-3.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:24.600
And now I've got
a chunk of code.

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:28.470
All I've done is one short
command line in the console

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.120
to create a new application.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:32.850
Let's make sure that that
has finished running.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:33.720
It has.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:34.470
OK.

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:37.070
And now what I'm
going to do is I

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:42.230
am going to deploy my
application to my new project.

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:44.870
So this is a
first-time deployment

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:46.280
of a brand new application.

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.639
And I built this application
on my local machine.

00:18:48.639 --> 00:18:50.930
I've got development tools
that Google makes available.

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.060
It's super easy to do.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:19:11.080
And once it's up and running,
if I go through to App Engine,

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:15.080
I should see the default
service is there.

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:19.010
I'm still waiting for the
API events service to run.

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:27.010
OK.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:28.050
I refresh here.

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:39.770
OK, I can see both
services are running.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:42.010
I can see that by default,
there are no instances

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:44.020
because nobody has
tried to access

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:45.970
either one of the services yet.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:51.270
And let's try out
the default service.

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:55.610
OK, so once the deployment
to our new project is done,

00:19:55.610 --> 00:19:57.570
we should be able
to click on this.

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:01.275
And if all goes well, we'll see
that the frontend is actually

00:20:01.275 --> 00:20:01.900
up and running.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:04.937
You'll notice that there's
no information for projects

00:20:04.937 --> 00:20:06.520
and renewals and
trending because this

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:07.790
is a brand new instance.

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:11.170
There's no data
in the Datastore,

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:13.330
so we would have to
populate the data.

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:15.910
But we can see that it
was actually as simple

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:19.360
as 25 seconds of waiting
with one command line

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:23.210
to actually deploy and get the
application up and running.

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:25.510
So with that, what
we've shown is

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:29.170
that it's super simple
to create a new project

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.710
and deploy a new application,
that that application can scale

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:36.490
servers up and down
automatically based on load,

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:39.100
that it's really easy
to upload new versions

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:42.580
and actually do partial
releases of those versions.

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:46.090
And you can easily
segregate your services

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:49.000
within an application so that
you can decouple the release

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:53.080
schedule of the API for
events and the frontend

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:55.900
for the end users.

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:57.050
Isn't that cool?

00:20:57.050 --> 00:21:00.060
Hopefully you saw
something you liked.

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:03.960
Let's switch back
to the presentation

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:07.800
and talk at a high level about
the wide range of functionality

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:11.780
available to you in App Engine.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:15.410
So as you've sussed
out by now, App Engine

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:18.230
is basically a magic
layer of software

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:22.880
that allows you to take business
logic and some application

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:28.670
code, upload it, and have it
run with little to no operations

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:32.750
overhead on top of Google's
massive compute storage

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:35.830
and networking infrastructure.

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:39.610
You don't have to worry
about defining servers,

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:42.200
standing them up,
tearing them down.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:44.740
You don't have to worry
replication, load balancing,

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:45.760
any of that stuff.

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:47.500
You can do all of this
in Compute Engine,

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:52.540
but in App Engine, it's all sort
of done for you with a software

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:56.280
layer that Google provides.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:58.420
Now, there are two
different environments

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:00.160
available in App Engine.

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:02.410
What we showed you in
that demo is what's

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:04.630
called standard environment.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:06.820
And what happens in
standard environment

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:08.260
is you take your code--

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:12.220
and when you deploy it, Google
creates a containerized version

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:16.990
of your application in a quite
locked-down and standardized

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.790
container environment.

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:20.710
They've got four
separate runtimes

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:23.200
that you can use with App
Engine standard environment.

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:26.615
There's Java,
Python, Go, and PHP.

00:22:26.615 --> 00:22:28.240
So if you want to
use another language,

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:29.830
standard environment isn't
going to work for you.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:31.746
Don't worry, there's
another environment which

00:22:31.746 --> 00:22:33.460
we'll talk about in a second.

00:22:33.460 --> 00:22:37.240
But the benefit of having this
really restricted, locked-down

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:39.910
container environment
is that Google can then

00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:42.760
apply all sorts of automation
and management magic

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:46.620
to reduce the operations
overhead on you.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:49.350
When you are using standard
environment runtimes,

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.594
you have access to a
bunch of support services.

00:22:51.594 --> 00:22:53.010
So for instance,
there's a service

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:55.260
that allows you to
send and receive emails

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:56.630
from your application.

00:22:56.630 --> 00:23:01.290
There's a service that allows
you to resize and manipulate

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:02.400
images.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.380
There's an API-- as
I noted-- that allows

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:07.410
you to do a shared
memcache across all

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:10.830
of your autoscaled
server instances.

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:13.150
These are all available to you.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:15.450
You can do all of this
stuff without using

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.670
the built-in standard
environment APIs,

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.590
but you would have to
load client libraries

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:22.360
and write a little
bit more code.

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:25.530
Now, the upside to these
very tightly constrained

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.739
standard environment runtimes
is all of the automation magic

00:23:28.739 --> 00:23:29.280
that happens.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:32.760
The downside is they create
some constraints which

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:36.330
are unfamiliar to programmers
coming from other environments.

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:40.000
So for instance, you can't
have background processes,

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:41.850
you can't write
to local disk, you

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:44.370
can't install
third-party runtimes.

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:47.370
And all of your requests
to your application

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:51.240
have to return and
complete within 60 seconds.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:55.400
So if you're a developer used to
working in other environments,

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:58.190
you're going to have to learn
some new patterns to make

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:00.470
your apps run in App
Engine standard environment

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:03.740
and take care of all of
the automation magic.

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:07.280
Now, Google understands
that for some developers,

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:09.800
this isn't a viable
solution, and so they've

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.440
created something called
flexible environment.

00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:14.210
And what flexible
environment does

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:18.140
is it gives you the ability to
use a wider range of runtimes

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:20.782
and to customize
your own runtimes.

00:24:20.782 --> 00:24:22.490
So you have to do a
little bit more work.

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:24.740
You have to actually
create a Docker

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:27.200
file that describes
the configuration

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:29.370
of your container.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:31.675
It does allow you to
do a variety of things

00:24:31.675 --> 00:24:32.550
you might want to do.

00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:34.890
So for instance, you can
support additional languages.

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:37.500
You can support Ruby
or Node or .NET.

00:24:37.500 --> 00:24:39.750
You can install
third-party binaries.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:41.250
You can do all the
programming stuff

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:43.333
that you're used to, like
accessing the local file

00:24:43.333 --> 00:24:46.200
system, writing to
the local file system,

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:47.460
that sort of thing.

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:52.140
You trade off, though,
access to some capabilities

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:53.310
in standard environment.

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:56.580
So for instance, you have
to do a little bit more

00:24:56.580 --> 00:24:58.860
overhead defining your
Docker containers,

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:00.630
configuring your runtimes.

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:03.510
Some of the APIs are
unavailable if you're

00:25:03.510 --> 00:25:06.210
using nonstandard runtimes.

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:10.350
And you cannot host your
flexible environment App Engine

00:25:10.350 --> 00:25:12.090
applications in Europe.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:14.836
You can host it in North
America or Asia Pacific.

00:25:14.836 --> 00:25:16.710
You can access it from
anywhere in the world,

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:19.961
but you just can't have the app
running in the European data

00:25:19.961 --> 00:25:20.460
centers.

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:27.240
What kind of instances does
your App Engine application

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:28.420
have to choose from?

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:32.160
So in standard environment,
your App Engine instance classes

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:36.720
range from the small end of a
128 megabytes and 600 megahertz

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:40.200
to one gigabyte of
memory and 4.8 gigahertz.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:43.800
In flexible environment, you
can use any of the standard GCE

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:46.470
machine types, so you
have a lot of flexibility

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:49.050
in terms of the
amount of processor

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.930
and the amount of memory.

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.750
In standard environment, you've
got three types of scaling.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:57.060
You've got automatic,
basic, and manual.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:00.900
So manual, you would choose
exactly how many instances

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:02.070
you want running.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:05.370
You would have to change the
configuration file and redeploy

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:07.890
in order to get more instances.

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:11.430
Automatic will use that
algorithm that we talked about.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:13.020
And basic is sort of in between.

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:17.370
Flexible environment also offers
automatic and basic scaling,

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:20.820
but it does scaling the same
way that GCE does autoscaling.

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:23.820
So that would be based
on CPU utilization

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:28.190
or on custom
Stackdriver metrics.

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:30.060
Billing can get a
little complicated.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:31.170
I've got a summary here.

00:26:31.170 --> 00:26:34.350
I would suggest that you
actually search on App Engine

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:35.880
billing calculator.

00:26:35.880 --> 00:26:38.060
And they've got a fairly
sophisticated calculator

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:39.810
where you can type in
all your parameters.

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:42.630
Or run test loads
and get an idea

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:44.190
of what the costs look like.

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:48.890
But the basic thing here is
for standard environment you

00:26:48.890 --> 00:26:50.190
get billed for instance hours.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:51.564
So you have no
instances running?

00:26:51.564 --> 00:26:53.630
You pay no instance hour fees.

00:26:53.630 --> 00:26:58.010
Instance hours range from $0.05
per hour to $0.40 per hour,

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:00.740
depending on the
instance class type.

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:03.510
And then if you are using
services-- so for instance,

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:05.780
if you're making
calls to the Datastore

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:08.030
or you're making calls
to the image API,

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:12.650
you may pay a fee
based on x number

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:15.260
of executions of the API.

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:17.450
Flex environment
pricing is comprised

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:21.200
of an hourly fee for the
number of CPUs you're using,

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:23.660
an hourly fee for the amount
of memory that you're using,

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:26.120
and an hourly fee for
whatever persistent disk you

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:29.120
have associated with
those instances.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:32.840
And then on top of
the instance charges,

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:36.830
you pay for Datastore use and
you pay for network egress.

00:27:36.830 --> 00:27:40.790
So any data that goes into your
App Engine application is free.

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:43.772
Any data that comes
out of the GCP region,

00:27:43.772 --> 00:27:44.980
you actually have to pay for.

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:51.110
And again, that's $0.08 to $0.11
per terabyte in North America

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:55.940
and a little bit more expensive,
say, in Australia or in China.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:58.250
We used the Google
Cloud data store--

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:00.680
in the demo application,
we used that to store

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:05.090
all the information about
the individual companies

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:08.330
and the projects and
the trending information

00:28:08.330 --> 00:28:09.470
and so forth.

00:28:09.470 --> 00:28:13.220
Cloud Datastore is a NoSQL
document database that's

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:16.580
designed to be very flexible.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.910
So it's sort of a
schemaless datastore,

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.350
and it's also high-performance.

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:23.750
So you can scale to millions
and millions of rows

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:25.520
and it performs quite well.

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:28.700
It supports atomic transaction,
high availability--

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:33.320
so your data is replicated
across multiple physical

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:36.650
resources within a region.

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:39.530
As a result, what that means
is that if a particular device

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:41.270
goes down or even
a zone goes down,

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:43.880
your application
is still available

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:47.510
and your data is still there.

00:28:47.510 --> 00:28:49.790
Massive scalability-- so
as I said, really fast

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:51.950
across many, many, many rows.

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:54.890
Your data is encrypted at rest.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:58.492
And it's a fully managed service
with no planned downtime.

00:28:58.492 --> 00:29:00.950
So you don't have to worry
about a single point of failure.

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:03.920
You don't have to worry about
it being down Saturday mornings

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:05.930
from 8:00 to 12:00 AM.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:08.060
Google takes care of
all of that for you

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:12.530
and your application runs
without interruption.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:14.420
In terms of architecting
your application,

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:16.460
App Engine provides
you a variety

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:20.750
of features that allow you to
be more intelligent in the way

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:24.510
that you implement, deploy,
and manage your applications.

00:29:24.510 --> 00:29:26.287
So we talked about services.

00:29:26.287 --> 00:29:28.370
The idea here is you can
take a single application

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:33.290
and break it down into 2, 5,
10, or more micro services.

00:29:33.290 --> 00:29:37.550
That allows individual teams
to develop each service

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:38.600
at a different pace.

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:41.510
And you can roll those
out at differing versions

00:29:41.510 --> 00:29:43.580
and differing times.

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:46.100
Once you roll out new
versions, you can roll forward,

00:29:46.100 --> 00:29:49.820
you can roll back, you can split
traffic between those versions.

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:51.777
What we would do at
Highfive, for instance--

00:29:51.777 --> 00:29:54.110
which is a videoconferencing
company that I used to work

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:54.650
at--

00:29:54.650 --> 00:29:59.570
we would actually roll out
new features to, say, 10%

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:02.540
of the user base, then
to 30% of the user base.

00:30:02.540 --> 00:30:04.250
And assuming nothing
caught on fire

00:30:04.250 --> 00:30:06.375
and there weren't any
screams, we would roll it out

00:30:06.375 --> 00:30:08.240
to the rest of the user base.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:10.880
If anything went wrong, we
could simply roll it back

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:13.010
to the previous version.

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:17.330
Task queues allow you to offload
longer running processes.

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:19.580
So we said, for instance,
that in standard environment

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:22.850
you have a 60-second time limit
to return a response to the end

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:23.720
user.

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:26.780
What you could do is get
a request from a user,

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:30.230
return a response immediately,
but put some long-running task

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:32.510
on a task queue and
then that can run for 5,

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:35.330
10 minutes in the background
without a problem.

00:30:35.330 --> 00:30:39.710
There's also a new feature
that was rolled out just

00:30:39.710 --> 00:30:43.460
in February of 2017 called
Cloud Endpoint Frameworks.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:48.200
And this is a mechanism by
which you can expose APIs using

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:50.900
Google's Open API cloud proxy.

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:54.020
And this provides better
security, better scalability,

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:57.410
standardized interface,
and automatically generated

00:30:57.410 --> 00:31:00.380
JavaScript clients that you can
include in your applications

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:03.920
to make use of your APIs.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:05.414
So, given all
those capabilities,

00:31:05.414 --> 00:31:06.830
whether it's
standard environment,

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:09.650
flexible environment, whether
you're using a backend data

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:12.710
store of Cloud Datastore
or you're using, say,

00:31:12.710 --> 00:31:14.990
BigQuery, the question that
you want to ask yourself

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:19.210
is, what can you do
with Google App Engine?

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:22.000
You may have heard of a company
called Snap, previously known

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:22.700
as Snapchat.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:25.750
What they were able
to do with App Engine

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:31.330
is grow within a few years
from 0 to 700 million

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:34.390
plus photos and videos
shared every day

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:37.870
with a very small development
team and an even smaller

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:39.220
operations team.

00:31:39.220 --> 00:31:42.100
They were able to focus
exclusively on business logic

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:44.950
and application functionality,
and the scale just

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:46.330
happened in the backend.

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:48.850
You may have seen that they've
made a forward commitment

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:52.180
to spend $2 billion on Google
Cloud Platform services

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:53.350
over the next five years.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:56.800
So, obviously, it's a
long-term bet for them.

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.470
Your application probably
isn't going to be this big,

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:02.780
but one can hope.

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:05.120
So let's say you leave
the session today

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:07.641
and as you're working with
App Engine you have questions

00:32:07.641 --> 00:32:09.140
or you need a little
bit of guidance

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:10.760
on how to do specific things.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:12.930
Where do you look?

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:14.940
So you start with search--

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:16.320
Google preferably.

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:19.320
And you can search for GAE--

00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:20.706
short for Google App Engine--

00:32:20.706 --> 00:32:21.830
and your topic of interest.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:25.080
So for instance, here
we've got GAE versions.

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:29.460
And the first link is
a link to App Engine

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:30.870
standard environment.

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:35.010
And then you've got the ability
to do deploying versions

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:36.490
and so forth.

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:39.140
There's also App Engine
and your topic of interest.

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:42.330
Sometimes GAE isn't
specific enough

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:46.620
and putting App Engine
will work better.

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:48.420
The documentation
is a little bit

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:51.810
different than what you have
seen with other Google Cloud

00:32:51.810 --> 00:32:53.040
Platform products.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:55.890
So you still at one point
end up with this nice table

00:32:55.890 --> 00:32:59.520
of contents, which has got
quick start, how-to guides, API

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:01.980
references, concepts,
tutorials, and so forth.

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:05.280
But there's a level of
organization above this--

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:09.440
and it would actually be helpful
if I just showed this to you.

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:12.575
So if we look at
Google App Engine--

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:16.630
I always start with search.

00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:19.370
I'm going to go to
App Engine platform.

00:33:19.370 --> 00:33:21.950
So this is the overview page.

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:25.880
If I click on View App Engine
Docs, what you'll notice

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:28.070
is it doesn't take me to
the table of contents.

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:31.130
Instead it takes me to a
table of contents that asks me

00:33:31.130 --> 00:33:33.870
what language I'm using.

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:36.120
And they've actually got
versions of the documentation

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:37.620
for all of the
popular languages.

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:40.230
So I use Python primarily.

00:33:40.230 --> 00:33:41.700
So if we look at
Python, then you

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:45.750
have the next level, which
is, choose your preferred

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:46.320
environment.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:49.140
Do you want flexible environment
or standard environment?

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:51.180
When you click
here, this will then

00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:54.540
take you to the familiar
table of contents section.

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:57.060
But they have a different
one of these pages

00:33:57.060 --> 00:34:01.800
and different subordinate
sections for each combination

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:04.384
of language and environment.

00:34:04.384 --> 00:34:06.300
So keep that in mind as
you're looking around.

00:34:08.830 --> 00:34:13.130
Now, as you're considering App
Engine for your environment,

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:15.350
a few things to consider.

00:34:15.350 --> 00:34:17.300
One, follow a process.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:20.290
Figure out what it is
you need to know in order

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:23.409
to make a decision
and what success is--

00:34:23.409 --> 00:34:25.330
what threshold you
need to cross in order

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:30.059
to determine that App Engine is,
in fact, the solution for you.

00:34:30.059 --> 00:34:31.600
Think about how
you're going to test.

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:34.360
Are you're going to lift
an existing application

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:36.010
and put it in App Engine?

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:38.770
The answer to that is
often no-- certainly not

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:41.110
for standard environment
because there

00:34:41.110 --> 00:34:43.810
are a lot of structural changes
that you might need to make.

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:45.909
You could lift an
application from

00:34:45.909 --> 00:34:47.949
a containerized environment
perhaps and put it

00:34:47.949 --> 00:34:51.010
on flexible environment.

00:34:51.010 --> 00:34:53.889
But figure out what you're
going to do-- probably

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:56.350
something like a
minimum viable product

00:34:56.350 --> 00:35:00.242
or a prototype of something new
that you're wanting to build.

00:35:00.242 --> 00:35:01.450
Figure out who's on the team.

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:05.680
You're going to need somebody
to design the test product.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:07.810
You're going to need
software engineers.

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:10.450
You're going to need
somebody to project manage

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:13.895
the entire evaluation process,
and then decision-makers

00:35:13.895 --> 00:35:15.520
to whom you report
the results and they

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:16.840
help make the decision.

00:35:16.840 --> 00:35:19.470
Note you need to
be comfortable--

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:22.450
or you need to get
designers or developers that

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:23.620
are comfortable--

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:26.710
in order to make this
evaluation worthwhile.

00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:28.690
If you're going
to get developers

00:35:28.690 --> 00:35:32.470
who are very entrenched in
another development methodology

00:35:32.470 --> 00:35:35.470
and they're not
particularly keen to learn

00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:39.620
how to work with App Engine's
specific constraints,

00:35:39.620 --> 00:35:42.340
then it's probably
not the right person

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:43.870
to have involved
in your evaluation

00:35:43.870 --> 00:35:46.540
or that's not the right
use case to attempt

00:35:46.540 --> 00:35:47.890
to move to App Engine.

00:35:47.890 --> 00:35:50.950
What you want is somebody
who's willing to pick up

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:54.550
the specific rules and patterns
for designing and developing

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:57.340
for App Engine in order to
get all the benefits out

00:35:57.340 --> 00:36:00.010
in the operations side.

00:36:00.010 --> 00:36:01.990
Again, follow a timeline.

00:36:01.990 --> 00:36:04.780
So typically, you can
make an application

00:36:04.780 --> 00:36:07.390
that proves out how
well App Engine will

00:36:07.390 --> 00:36:09.352
work for you within a month.

00:36:09.352 --> 00:36:11.670
If it's more complicated,
it'll take longer.

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:13.860
If you have more people,
it'll take less time.

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:15.900
And you want to break it
down into three phases.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:18.030
There's a phase where
you define your criteria

00:36:18.030 --> 00:36:19.530
and you recruit
your team and you

00:36:19.530 --> 00:36:21.960
agree on your high-level tests.

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:25.410
There's a section of time
where you do some research,

00:36:25.410 --> 00:36:27.900
you do some point
testing, you make

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:29.970
sure you actually understand
how App Engine works

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:31.230
and how you're going
to build your thing.

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:32.130
You build it.

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:34.170
And then ultimately
you perform your tests.

00:36:34.170 --> 00:36:37.260
And you're testing
probably for performance,

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:41.000
you're testing for functionality
and developer productivity,

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:42.660
you're testing for pricing.

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:44.670
And then you collect
all of those results,

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:47.430
you analyze them, and
you make a decision.

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:50.100
And hopefully this
gives you a sense

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:52.260
of all the things you
can do with App Engine

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:55.650
and how you can try App
Engine out for your use cases

00:36:55.650 --> 00:36:57.840
to make a good decision.

00:36:57.840 --> 00:36:59.340
And that's it for this session.

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:00.790
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:02.748
I hope you learned
something and that you found

00:37:02.748 --> 00:37:04.680
it interesting and useful.

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:07.680
We'll be sending you a follow-up
email that has a link to a code

00:37:07.680 --> 00:37:11.520
lab so you can get some guided
experience with App Engine

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:12.894
yourself.

00:37:12.894 --> 00:37:14.310
We'd love to get
your feedback, so

00:37:14.310 --> 00:37:17.580
feel free to provide that
feedback on the screen.

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:20.330
And stay tuned for
our next session.

